<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Truth and Reconciliation Commission (South Africa)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1995–2002</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/truth-and-reconciliation-commission-peru">Truth and Reconciliation Commission</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a> was set up in 1995 after the end of apartheid to investigate gross human rights violations committed between 1960 and 1994. Chaired by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/desmond-tutu">Archbishop Desmond Tutu</a>, it held public hearings from 1996 in which victims testified and perpetrators could receive amnesty if they made full disclosure of politically motivated crimes. Its report, presented from 1998, became a model for truth commissions elsewhere.</p></div>
